agencies, while authorizing state governments to bring suit over immigration enforcement."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, generating demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill requires the Department of Homeland Security to detain certain non-citizens who are in the country unlawfully and have been arrested for or charged with crimes including burglary, theft, assault on law enforcement, or offenses causing death or serious injury. It also allows state governments to sue the federal government if immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing detainees, not conducting required asylum interviews, or violating parole limitations—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001061","score":0.020741019411576217},"overlapCount":49},"meaning":{"percentile":56.91044068867478,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.9088554574461727}},{"billId":"119-s-331","bioguideId":"H001089","cycle":2026,"score":0.008832041555509103,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":56365.7,"donorShare":0.02618754755740583,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.008057706940740256},{"industry":"education","donorAmount":6500,"donorShare":0.003019904997598502,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0007743346147688467}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.9088554574461727},"bill":{"id":"119-s-331","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"331","title":"HALT Fentanyl Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-30","latestActionDate":"2025-07-16","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-26.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Justice","Drug trafficking and controlled substances","Licensing and registrations","Research administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001075","cosponsorCount":30,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/331","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes registration and licensing requirements for pharmaceutical researchers conducting controlled substance research and affects the regulatory classification of chemical substances."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill establishes new criminal penalties and quantity thresholds that will affect prosecution and defense of drug trafficking cases."},{"industry":"education","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Universities and research institutions conducting controlled substance research would be subject to the new registration processes and requirements established by the bill."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would permanently classify all fentanyl-related substances as Schedule I controlled substances, meaning they would be treated as having high abuse potential and no accepted medical use. It sets the same criminal penalties and quantity thresholds for fentanyl-related substances as currently apply to fentanyl analogues. The bill also creates a streamlined registration process for researchers studying Schedule I substances and relaxes some requirements for research facilities working with controlled substances.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","confidence":